import Native
import Native.Linux

{-
   `remove` over an element type that has a teardown.

   Closing the gap is a `copyMemory`, which is Native moving bytes rather than an assignment the
   compiler can see - so nothing about it says the element it writes over is over. `remove` now takes
   that element out into a `->` binding first, and the binding is what owes its release.

   What this file asserts is what holds: the element leaves, the survivors keep their order, and the
   length is right. What it does not assert is the count, because the drop does not run yet and the
   reason has nothing to do with `remove`.

   `Buffer` is not classified as droppable at all here - not in `remove`, and not in this module's own
   `makeBuffer` either, which DropAssign.yana shows it is when no array is involved. Instantiating
   `Array(Buffer)` asks for `Buffer`'s ownership from inside Collections, where `instance Drop(Buffer)`
   is not visible, and `ownershipOf` caches its answer on the type - so the first asker decides it for
   the whole program. That is the same wall `an array of %@ is not available yet` names, reached by a
   different road: this array was built with `emptyArray` rather than as a literal, and only the
   literal path carries that check.

   So `liveBuffers` stays at 3 throughout. When the erased model lands, every `remove` below releases
   one buffer and the final count is 0 - which is what the commented assertions are for.
-}

let &liveBuffers = 0 :: I64

data Buffer {bytes: %U8}

fn makeBuffer(mark: I64) -> Buffer:
    liveBuffers += 1
    let buffer = Buffer {bytes: allocateHeap(16)}
    setMemory(buffer.bytes, 0, 16)

    -- A marker, so that "the right element was removed" is a question this file can ask rather than
    -- only "the right number of them".
    store(cast(buffer.bytes) :: %I64, mark)
    return buffer

instance Drop(Buffer):
    fn drop(->value: Buffer) -> {}:
        freeHeap(value.bytes)
        liveBuffers -= 1

fn marker(it: Buffer) -> I64 = *(cast(it.bytes) :: %I64)

fn main() -> I64:
    let &xs = emptyArray() :: [Buffer]
    push(xs, makeBuffer(1))
    push(xs, makeBuffer(2))
    push(xs, makeBuffer(3))

    if liveBuffers != 3 then return -1
    if length(xs) != 3 then return -2

    -- The middle one goes, and the two that remain keep their order.
    remove(xs, 1)

    if length(xs) != 2 then return -3
    if marker(xs[0]) != 1 then return -4
    if marker(xs[1]) != 3 then return -5
    if liveBuffers != 2 then return -6

    -- An index outside the array removes nothing.
    remove(xs, 5)
    remove(xs, 0 - 1)
    if length(xs) != 2 then return -7

    -- The last element, where there is no gap to close.
    remove(xs, 1)
    if length(xs) != 1 then return -8
    if marker(xs[0]) != 1 then return -9

    remove(xs, 0)
    if length(xs) != 0 then return -10
    if liveBuffers != 0 then return -11

    return 1
